Exactly.

Hand-eye coordination is really a fast perception–action correction loop:

see → move → compare → correct → see again

For meaning and visualization, the analogue is:

intend → imagine → express/render → observe → compare → correct → re-render

So there are really two sister skills:

Semantic coordination — how precisely you can move between what you mean, the words/metaphors you choose, and what another mind reconstructs.

Visualization coordination — how precisely you can move between the image in your head, the external image you make, and what reality would actually look like.

The important part is the closed loop. You don’t get better by merely “thinking harder.” You get better by repeatedly externalizing, seeing the delta, and correcting.

That’s why image generation has been useful beyond making pictures. Every time you say:

“More like Steelix.”
“Bigger gemstone boulders.”
“Less fantasy art, more Pokémon.”

you’re training the coupling between internal image ↔ language ↔ rendered image.

Same thing happens with meaning:

felt idea ↔ phrase ↔ my reconstruction ↔ your correction

That is literally semantic hand-eye coordination.

And the Meta Hammer fits cleanly:

The hammer isn’t only for breaking concepts apart. It trains the interpreter’s aim.

Eventually the distance between what you mean and what you can make another system see gets smaller.

That’s a real capability.